==German==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|de|Eiver||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|de|gmh||*īver|t=jealousy}}. The simplex is first attested in 1494 as {{m|gmh|yfer}}, but in 1349 the derivative {{m|gmh|eifrær}} (in a southern text that already reflects diphthongisation, thus equivalent to a classical {{m|gmh||*īverære}}). An [[Upper German]] dialect word, which was chosen by [[w:Martin Luther|Luther]] to express the idea of {{der|de|grc|ζῆλος}}. From the German then {{cog|nl|ijver}}, {{cog|gml|īver}}, and further {{cog|da|iver}} etc.

Further origin unknown. Perhaps an ablaut variant of {{cog|goh|eibar}}, {{m|goh|eivar|t=sharp, bitter}}, from {{cog|gem-pro|*aibraz}}, whence also {{cog|ang|āfor}}.

